    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1228">

                <pb facs="1165" n="1153" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg1153"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.3310/1605½</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to President Roosevelt</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-01">June 1, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: With reference to the
                    attached telegram from our Minister in Montevideo,<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d1228-f1"/> I realize that the specific suggestion <pb facs="1166"
                        n="1154" xml:id="pg1154"/> made is impracticable. However, I do feel
                    strongly that something more is required than a visit every ten days or so by
                    one of our naval vessels. I still feel that if some way can be found by which at
                    least three or four heavy cruisers and a reasonable number of destroyers can be
                    kept on the East Coast this summer, the insurance value of such a step will be
                    very great.</p>
                <p>May I ask if it is not possible to let at least another two cruisers be sent to
                    those waters, bringing them from Pearl Harbor for that purpose.</p>
                <p>Believe me</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sumner Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
